hi everybody i hope we're all doing well  um  some of my favorite moments in every  stormlight book so far  happens within the last hundred pages uh  that's not to say there aren't great  moments  in the rest of the book you go in  knowing  okay there's going to be a lot of build  up and things are going to progress to a  point where it's just so much happening  all at once  pacing in fantasy books in general  there are different expectations you  know you don't go into epic fantasy  expecting thriller  pace or you know a why a romance  expecting  a fantasy pace you know going into a  fantasy book there's going to be  likely many characters multiple points  of view traditional fantasy  with a huge emphasis on world building  you know you don't really go into  reading a fantasy book  unless you enjoy world building on some  level and  epic fantasy when you are consciously  making huge  gigantic books which sanderson is doing  with the stormlight archive that changes  things too  these conceptions about pacing and  fantasy  make it really interesting and make it  an interesting problem for  for writers to figure out how to address  and keep the story moving and keep a  sense of progression while also  delivering on all of this extra stuff  and i think that generally sanderson is  excellent at doing this  and i think a perfect comparison that  can be made  is with words of radiance and  oathbringer  so i think that in general you could  talk about pacing on  a small scale and pacing on a very large  scale  sanderson writes chapters in the  stormlight archive that are  short stories almost and you see that in  the in the interlude chapters  each chapter often feels like a short  story in itself  often they're self-contained often  they're uh sometimes a chapter is meant  just to explore the world  we want to see some kind of character  interaction some kind of  goal achieved or twist and i think  that's done  really well however it doesn't feel like  there's a lot of continuity from chapter  chat to chapter  and this is really prevalent in  oathbringer especially when the point of  view  characters start to expand and expand  and expand and i personally  felt like i could read a chapter one  chapter of oathbringer and go  i'm good for the day you know that had a  nice little arc  in that chapter more of a leisurely pace  you know you  want to take your time exploring this  book that's fine but it  becomes hard when the whole macro level  plot doesn't get resolved until the last  couple of chapters  and especially when you've become used  to that formula in the first two books  you know like okay there's going to be a  lot of  treading water part of my problem with  oathbringer is that i was really hyper  conscious of it  i i could feel like okay this is gonna  be delayed  until the very end and then this this  thread of  of plot is not going to coincide with  this thread of plot  until the very end and it started to  feel a little bit  more convenient in words of radiance  there's the ticking  clock so the final part i want to  discuss is divisions  of progress just so that for the sake of  getting into more detail into this i'm  going to be talking about spoilers until  this time so if you don't want to hear  spoilers just jump to  for for oathbringer just jump to this  time and um  there you go you know trying to liberate  the town of kolinar  this whole sequence is the is so  interesting to me because i feel like  building up to it i was so excited i was  like man this is going to be great we  have all of our characters  that we love together you know there's  going to be some interesting tension  between shalon and khalid and  adeline and and elokar you know is  trying to redeem himself  but we're totally left out of the loop  and then when we get there you know it  kind of reminds me of like the build up  to a great  to like an action movie or um in aliens  the the military is coming back and  ripley's with them and they know  something bad is going on on the planet  you know they don't know exactly what it  is yet  but they know something's bad it felt  like as soon as they got to culinary  it became describing you know exactly  how this town is working  all of this stuff and as soon as the  characters get there seemed like they  all split off  they kind of had their own little side  quests as soon as they got there and  especially when it felt like okay our  characters have kind of been separate  from each other now this is the moment  when they can be together  progress is all divided into little  things and they're all separated  we have this goal but suddenly we have  these little side stories  that have gotten in the way and  distracted us well another example is  shadesmar which happens right after the  culinary stuff there's a lot of focus on  just describing this whole new world  and figuring out how it works which can  be really fun but  when story threads are unresolved when  it feels like all of the conflict and  tension that happened in culinary just  put on pause  and we're just like going what's what's  going on we don't know we're left in the  dark  it feels frustrating in some ways it was  just  it was a massive book with three  different books inside of it or four  different books inside of it and that's  my problem  somewhat with oathbringer however it's  hard to even blame it because  i feel like that's like a herculean task  i think pacing in novels is one of the  most like under discussed  topics the pacing of a scene the pacing  of a paragraph of a sentence you know  is just so hard sometimes to  sense and hear and and get a grip on  sometimes  the pacing of a chapter might be great  which i feel like sanderson's  chapter pacing is wonderful and he has  wonderful little arcs within each  chapter and section but brought all  together  some things were way over developed and  over discussed and some things were  really underdeveloped you know it's a  topic i wanted 